[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"MongoDB Change Streams: Events and Operations"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"MongoDB change streams provide real-time data access to database changes. Applications can use change streams to react to data insertions, updates, deletes, and other modifications as they occur. This tutorial covers the fundamental concepts of change stream events and operations in MongoDB."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Change Stream Events"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A change stream emits events that describe modifications to data. Each event contains information about the operation that occurred, the affected document, and other metadata. The structure of a change stream event is consistent across different types of operations."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":" '_id': A unique identifier for the change stream event."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":" 'operationType': The type of operation that triggered the event (e.g., 'insert', 'update', 'delete', 'replace', 'invalidate')."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":" 'ns': An object containing the database name ('db') and collection name ('coll')."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":" 'documentKey': An object containing the '_id' of the document affected by the operation."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":" 'updateDescription': (For 'update' operations) An object describing the fields that were updated or removed."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":" 'fullDocument': (For 'insert', 'replace', and 'update' operations) The document after the change. The availability of this field depends on the 'fullDocument' option specified when opening the change stream."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Common Change Stream Operations"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here are some common operations that trigger change stream events:"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Insert: A new document is inserted into a collection."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Update: An existing document is modified."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Replace: An existing document is completely replaced with a new document."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Delete: A document is removed from a collection."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Invalidate: Indicates that the change stream is no longer valid (e.g., due to a collection drop or rename)."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Opening a Change Stream"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"To open a change stream, use the 'watch()' method on a MongoClient, a database, or a collection. The 'watch()' method returns a change stream cursor that you can iterate over to receive events."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"const { MongoClient } = require('mongodb');

async function main() {
  const uri = "mongodb://localhost:27017";
  const client = new MongoClient(uri);

  try {
    await client.connect();

    const db = client.db('mydatabase');
    const collection = db.collection('mycollection');

    const changeStream = collection.watch();

    changeStream.on('change', next => {
      console.log('Change detected:\n', next);
    });

    // Perform database operations here (e.g., insert, update, delete)
    await collection.insertOne({ name: 'Example Document' });

    // Keep the connection alive to continue receiving changes
    await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 5000));

  } finally {
    await client.close();
  }
}

main().catch(console.error);"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Filtering Change Stream Events"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can filter change stream events by providing a pipeline to the 'watch()' method. The pipeline is an array of aggregation stages that specify which events to include in the stream. This allows you to listen for specific operations or changes to particular documents."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"const changeStream = collection.watch([
  { $match: { 'operationType': 'update' } }
]);"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"The above example filters the change stream to only include 'update' operations."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Resuming a Change Stream"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Change streams can be resumed after an interruption (e.g., network failure or server restart). To resume a change stream, you need to store the resume token ('_id' field of the last processed event) and provide it to the 'watch()' method when reopening the stream."},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"const resumeToken = '...'; // Stored resume token
const changeStream = collection.watch([], { resumeAfter: resumeToken });"},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"Considerations"},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Change streams require a replica set or sharded cluster."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The 'fullDocument' option can impact performance, as it requires MongoDB to retrieve the full document for each event."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Proper error handling and resume token management are crucial for reliable change stream processing."}]
